I"#<h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>

<p>My undergraduate thesis applied state-of-the-art machine learning to predict crop yield for Ethiopian agricultural regions — a high-impact problem where accurate forecasts aid food security planning.</p>

<h2 id="dataset">Dataset</h2>

<p>Curated a dataset from multiple sources: satellite imagery indices (NDVI), weather station records, soil composition data, and historical yield records. Combined automated scripting (Python + Pandas) with manual verification and STATA for statistical analysis.</p>

<h2 id="model">Model</h2>

<p>Trained and evaluated several ML models, with a final <strong>TensorFlow Deep Learning predictor</strong> (MLP with attention over feature groups) outperforming classical baselines (Random Forest, XGBoost) on held-out test regions.</p>

<h2 id="publication">Publication</h2>

<p>Results and methodology published in the <strong>Addis Ababa University Journal of Engineering</strong>. This was among the first deep learning applications to this specific regional crop yield problem.</p>
